WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.029 --> 00:00:03.589
Why are they taking the machines?

00:00:03.590 --> 00:00:04.849
We got busted.

00:00:04.850 --> 00:00:06.529
It never even occurred to me that

00:00:06.530 --> 00:00:08.419
the game was illegal.

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:09.919
But for over three decades, pinball

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:11.929
was banned in New York City.

00:00:11.930 --> 00:00:13.699
Most people have no idea that

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:15.589
pinball was illegal in New

00:00:15.590 --> 00:00:17.509
York from the early 1940s

00:00:17.510 --> 00:00:19.759
until 1976,

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:21.979
when a journalist named Roger Sharpe

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:23.959
finally won his crusade against

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:26.479
the city to free the flippers.

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:28.549
The story of that insane ban

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:30.259
is the subject of the new movie

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:32.149
Pinball: The Man Who Saved

00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:33.979
the Game, which Richard Brody

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:35.839
of The New Yorker called "better

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:37.819
than all ten of the Best Picture

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:39.709
nominees." The film was written

00:00:39.710 --> 00:00:41.299
and directed by Austin Bragg and

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:43.429
Meredith Bragg, long-time producers

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:44.959
at Reason, best known for

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:46.369
collaborating with Remy on his

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:48.769
massively popular song parodies

00:00:48.770 --> 00:00:50.779
and for making libertarian versions

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:52.729
of Star Trek, Star Wars, Game

00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:54.979
of Thrones and other pop-culture

00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:55.980
franchises.

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:01.239
Who you're sending holograms to

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:03.039
is information about you and your

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:04.059
personal life.

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:05.619
Are you telling me the crown is 3

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:06.219
million in debt?

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:07.869
Actually, it's 20 trillion.

00:01:07.870 --> 00:01:09.099
How could you let this happen?

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:10.929
Entitlement spending, a bunch of

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:12.819
wars, that bullet train to

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:13.820
Riverrun didn't help.

00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:16.869
How did you hear about Roger

00:01:16.870 --> 00:01:17.989
Sharpe and this story?

00:01:17.990 --> 00:01:19.869
Because it's, I mean, it's

00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:21.729
just, it's a great story that

00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:24.039
this at first freelance journalist

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:25.959
for GQ ended up kind

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:28.089
of changing a whole legal reshape.

00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:29.559
I cold e-mailed Roger.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:30.939
I got on the phone with him and he

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:33.669
told me about all the other things.

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:35.829
Meeting a single mother

00:01:35.830 --> 00:01:37.149
and starting a relationship with

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:39.609
her, his time at GQ,

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:41.289
his back story.

00:01:41.290 --> 00:01:43.149
That made it more universal.

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:45.099
It was a lot more than just

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:46.959
essentially a sports movie about one

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:48.969
person making a particular shot.

00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:51.099
And about 3 hours later,

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:53.499
I hung up and I texted Austin

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:54.759
and I said, "I think this might be a

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:55.760
feature."

00:01:56.350 --> 00:01:57.879
This was the first pinball machine

00:01:57.880 --> 00:01:59.769
I'd found in the city.

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:01.479
It never even occurred to me that

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:02.679
the game was illegal.

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:04.810
I just knew I needed to play.

00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:08.709
Fiorello La Guardia is

00:02:08.710 --> 00:02:10.538
kind of the villain of the piece.

00:02:10.539 --> 00:02:13.089
Why did La Guardia ban pinball?

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:15.159
Yeah, La Guardia came in definitely

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:17.259
on a crusade to stop organized

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:19.179
crime, which I think

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:20.349
was a laudable goal.

00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:22.029
But it's, it was very hard to do and

00:02:22.030 --> 00:02:23.889
it's very hard to to show

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:26.289
people that he was succeeding.

00:02:26.290 --> 00:02:28.149
In 1933, Fiorello La Guardia

00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:29.679
ran for mayor, promising to rid New

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:30.939
York City of corruption and

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:32.079
organized crime.

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:33.579
Pinball, he said, was gambling,

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:35.409
ruled by the mob, a racket to steal

00:02:35.410 --> 00:02:36.339
lunch money.

00:02:36.340 --> 00:02:39.099
So he banned it, you know,

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:40.509
for the children.

00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:41.889
And it wasn't just a ban.

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:43.929
It was a public relations crusade.

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:45.189
Major raids throughout the city,

00:02:45.190 --> 00:02:46.749
squads of police swarming into

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:48.459
bowling alleys, bars, anywhere they

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:49.539
could find them.

